2186069732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069858 #edb/ref 1061 4770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069858 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c18f2e00-ae66-5fd4-ab0a-9e795845b07b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069858 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069869 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069869 #edb/ref 1061 4905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069869 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "65cce613-c74c-5057-9966-c834e52edbdf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069869 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069927 #edb/ref 1064 "To simplify a rational expression, we cancel out the largest common factor from the numerator and denominator. To do this, we sometimes need to factor the numerator and denominator before canceling.\n\nFor example, suppose that we wish to simplify $\\dfrac {4x - 12} {10x + 4}.$ To do this, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any factors that they have in common.\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {4x - 12} {10x + 4}\n\t\t&=\\dfrac {4(x - 3)} {2(5x + 2)}\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac {2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (x - 3)} {2(5x + 2)}\\[5pt]\t\t\n\t\t&=\\dfrac {\\cancel{2} \\cdot 2(x - 3)} {\\cancel{2}(5x + 2)}\\[5pt]\t\n\t\t&=\\dfrac {2(x - 3)} {5x + 2}\n\t}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069927 #edb/ref 1065 1370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069927 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "332f534f-811e-5c03-8393-560134dd4ea8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069927 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
